   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Aadhaar's Minimal Design: Collect Only What You Need\n\n**One-line hook:** To build an ID for over a billion people, the team chose to ask for less information, not more.\n\n## The Story\nWhen Nandan Nilekani led the Aadhaar project, many people wanted the new ID database to hold lots of data: caste, religion, income, and more. Every ministry had a wish list. Nilekani and the team pushed back.\n\nAadhaar collected a **minimal set of data**: basic details like name, date of birth, gender and address, plus **biometrics** (fingerprints, iris scans and a photo) to make sure each person was unique. It did not record caste, religion or income. Aadhaar was designed to do **one job well: prove you are who you say you are**. Other systems, such as banks or welfare schemes, could build on top of it.\n\nThe authors explain that keeping it minimal made it **simpler, faster to roll out, and less risky**. Less data meant fewer chances for misuse and fewer political fights about what the government was tracking.\n\n## The Lesson\nA system that tries to do everything does nothing well. **Keep the core small and focused**, and let others build on top of it.\n\n## When to Use This\n- Stakeholders keep adding \"must-have\" features\n- Designing a database, form or platform\n- Thinking about privacy and data risk\n- Building a foundation that others will rely on\n\n## Try This\n- For every field or feature, ask: \"What breaks if we leave this out?\" Cut what doesn't break anything.\n- Define the one job your system must do perfectly, and write it down.\n\n## Related\n- [[Nexus]]: the power and danger of information systems\n- [[No Place to Hide]]: what happens when governments collect too much\n- [[The 80/20 Principle]]: focus on the vital few\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What is the smallest version of this that still does the core job?\"*"
