   "body": "# From Tembhli to a Billion: Scaling Aadhaar Through Partners\n\n**One-line hook:** Aadhaar didn't scale by hiring a giant government army; it scaled by letting many partners do the enrolling.\n\n## The Story\nThe first Aadhaar numbers were handed out in **2010** in **Tembhli**, a small tribal village in Maharashtra. Starting in a poor, rural place was symbolic. Aadhaar was meant to give an identity to people who had often been left out of the system.\n\nThe bigger challenge was **scale**: enrolling hundreds of millions of people across a huge and diverse country. Instead of building one large government machine to do every enrollment, the authority (UIDAI) set **standards and technology**, and let an **ecosystem of \"registrars\"** do the work. These included state governments, banks and other agencies, which hired enrollment agencies on the ground. UIDAI kept a small core team and focused on the central system, especially **de-duplication** through biometrics.\n\nThis approach let enrollment grow fast, with many players running in parallel. The authors present it as a model for building public systems: **a thin core with open standards, and many partners at the edge.**\n\n## The Lesson\nTo scale fast, **don't do everything yourself**. Own the standards and the core, and let partners handle the rest.\n\n## When to Use This\n- Your team is the bottleneck for a big rollout\n- Designing a platform others will build on\n- Deciding what to own and what to outsource\n- Launching something symbolic to show who it's for\n\n## Try This\n- Split your project into \"core we must control\" and \"edge others can do.\" Write clear standards for the edge.\n- Choose a first launch location or user that sends a message about who the work is for.\n\n## Related\n- [[The Goal]]: removing the constraint to increase throughput\n- [[The Airbnb Story]]: platforms scaling through others' supply\n- [[The Toyota Way]]: standards make it possible to scale quality\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What am I doing myself that I should be enabling others to do?\"*"
