   "body": "# Three Air Mattresses and a Rent Problem: How Airbnb Began\n\n**One-line hook:** Two designers who couldn't pay their rent noticed that every hotel in town was sold out.\n\n## The Story\nIn 2007, **Brian Chesky** and **Joe Gebbia**, two friends from the Rhode Island School of Design, were sharing an apartment in San Francisco and struggling to pay the rent. A big **design conference** was coming to the city, and the hotels were booked up.\n\nThey had an idea: blow up some **air mattresses** on their floor and rent them to conference visitors, with breakfast included. They made a simple website, called it something like \"Air Bed & Breakfast,\" and got **three guests**. The guests were different people from different backgrounds, and the experience went well. The founders realized they had earned real money and, more importantly, that **strangers were willing to stay in someone's home**.\n\nThey later brought in **Nathan Blecharczyk**, a former roommate of Gebbia's, as the technical co-founder. The early years were hard and many investors passed. But the seed was planted by solving their own problem in a moment when demand far outran supply.\n\n## The Lesson\n**Big ideas often start as a scrappy fix to your own problem.** Look for moments when demand is clearly bigger than supply, and test a tiny version right away.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You're looking for a business or side-project idea\n- You have a personal problem that others probably share\n- You want to test an idea without building much\n- You see a market that's overloaded at certain moments\n\n## Try This\n- List three problems you've had this month and ask who else has them.\n- Run a weekend-sized test of your idea with real people paying real money.\n\n## Related\n- [[Shoe Dog]]: selling shoes out of a car trunk\n- [[Zero to One]]: finding a secret others don't see\n- [[The Undercover Economist]]: how scarcity creates pricing power\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What problem of my own could be someone else's paid solution?\"*"
