   "verified": false,
   "body": "# The White Knight That Turned Shareholders Against Arcelor\n\n**One-line hook:** To block Mittal, Arcelor's board rushed into a deal with a Russian steel tycoon, and its own shareholders rebelled.\n\n## The Story\nAs Mittal Steel pressed its hostile bid, Arcelor's management looked for a way out. They found a **\"white knight\"**: the Russian steelmaker **Severstal**, controlled by **Alexei Mordashov**. Arcelor announced a merger that would give Mordashov a large stake in the combined company, structured in a way that made it hard for shareholders to block.\n\nThe move was meant to stop Mittal. Instead, it angered Arcelor's **shareholders**. Many felt management was protecting itself and choosing a partner over the owners' heads, swapping one large controlling holder for another, without giving them a real say. The Severstal deal also looked worse for them than Mittal's improved offer.\n\nInvestor pressure grew until Arcelor's board changed course. It accepted a **raised Mittal offer**, which also came with governance commitments to address European concerns, and the Severstal deal was dropped. Mittal won partly because Arcelor's leaders had **lost the trust of their own owners**.\n\n## The Lesson\n**Leaders who forget whom they work for lose their support.** A defensive move that serves management instead of the people it answers to can collapse fast.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You're under pressure and tempted to make a quick defensive deal\n- Your decision affects stakeholders who aren't in the room\n- You're judging whether a leader is acting for the group or for themselves\n- You're structuring a deal and need others' buy-in\n\n## Try This\n- Before a big defensive move, ask: \"Would the people I answer to approve this if I explained it plainly?\"\n- Map each stakeholder's incentives, and check whether your plan actually serves them.\n\n## Related\n- [[Freakonomics]]: how incentives shape behavior\n- [[Extreme Ownership]]: leading with the mission, not self-protection\n- [[The World for Sale]]: deal-making in global commodities\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Is this move protecting the people I serve, or protecting me?\"*"
