   "body": "# Don't Ask a Brickmaker About Jewels\n\n**One-line hook:** Arkad's first year of savings vanished because he trusted the wrong expert.\n\n## The Story\nAfter a year of saving one-tenth of his earnings, young **Arkad** wanted to invest. He gave his savings to **Azmur, a brickmaker**, who was traveling to buy rare jewels from the **Phoenicians**. The plan was to sell them in Babylon for a profit.\n\nThe Phoenicians cheated Azmur. The \"jewels\" turned out to be **worthless pieces of glass**, and Arkad lost everything. When he told Algamish, the moneylender laughed at him: why would you trust a brickmaker's knowledge about jewels? You would go to a **jeweler** for advice about jewels, just as you'd ask a brickmaker about bricks.\n\nArkad learned the lesson and started over. This mistake appears again in the book's **laws of gold**: gold slips away from those who invest in businesses they don't understand, or follow advice from people who are not skilled in that field.\n\n## The Lesson\n**Take advice only from people who know the field.** Confidence and good intentions are not expertise.\n\n## When to Use This\n- A friend or relative pitches an investment\n- Getting advice outside someone's area of experience\n- Hiring someone for a job they haven't done before\n- Evaluating \"hot tips\" online or at work\n\n## Try This\n- Before acting on advice, ask: \"Has this person actually done this successfully, many times?\"\n- Keep a rule: never invest in something you can't explain in two sentences.\n\n## Related\n- [[Freakonomics]]: experts and information gaps\n- [[Talking to Strangers]]: why we trust people we shouldn't\n- [[The Data Detective]]: checking claims before believing them\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Am I asking a brickmaker about jewels?\"*"
