   "body": "# The Beatles in Hamburg & the 10,000-Hour Rule\n\n**One-line hook:** Before they were famous, the Beatles played eight-hour sets in Hamburg strip clubs, and that's where they got good.\n\n## The Story\nIn the early 1960s, the Beatles were an ordinary band that got booked to play in **Hamburg, Germany**. Back home they were used to short sets. In Hamburg they had to play for **hours a night, many nights a week**. Over a few trips they played a huge number of live shows, more than most bands play in an entire career.\n\nGladwell connects this to research by psychologist **Anders Ericsson**: the top performers in a field had typically put in about **10,000 hours of practice**. He also tells the story of **Bill Gates**, who as a teenager in 1968 got rare access to a computer terminal at his school (Lakeside) and logged thousands of hours of programming before almost anyone else could.\n\nThe point: genius usually comes from **lots of practice made possible by an unusual opportunity**.\n\n## The Lesson\nMastery takes huge amounts of practice, and *access to the chance to practice* is often the hidden advantage.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You feel behind compared to a \"natural\"\n- You're planning how to build a deep skill\n- You're judging someone's success and want to see the hidden opportunity behind it\n\n## Try This\n- Find your \"Hamburg\": a setting that forces many reps under pressure.\n- When you admire someone, ask: \"What unusual access did they get?\"\n\n## Related\n- [[Range]]: **debate**. Epstein argues that sampling widely first often beats early specialization outside \"kind\" learning environments like chess and golf.\n- [[Hidden Potential]]: how deliberate practice can be designed\n- [[The 1% Rule]]: small, steady gains that add up\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Where can I get 10x more practice than everyone else, safely and cheaply?\"*"
