   "body": "# Big Bazaar: A Supermarket That Felt Like a Bazaar\n\n**One-line hook:** Biyani made his stores less neat on purpose, because that's how Indians like to shop.\n\n## The Story\nWhen modern retail came to India, the obvious model was the **Western supermarket**: clean aisles, neat shelves, quiet shopping. Kishore Biyani thought this felt foreign to most Indian shoppers. They were used to **crowded markets (mandis)**, noise, bargains, and touching and checking goods before buying.\n\nSo with **Big Bazaar**, he designed stores to feel familiar. They were busy and full, with piles of products and a sense of a market. In **Food Bazaar**, staples such as grains and pulses could be shown loose in open bins so customers could **see and feel** them, as they would at a local shop. The goal was a store where an ordinary Indian family felt at home, not out of place.\n\nBiyani says much of his understanding came from **watching customers**, not from foreign consultants or textbooks. He wanted \"Indian\" retail, not a copy of the West.\n\n## The Lesson\nDon't copy a model that worked elsewhere. **Design for how your customers actually behave**, even if it looks \"less professional\" to outsiders.\n\n## When to Use This\n- Bringing a product or process from one country or culture to another\n- Your polished design isn't connecting with real users\n- Competing with a big, foreign or \"best practice\" player\n- Choosing between what experts recommend and what users do\n\n## Try This\n- Watch real customers use your product or service for an hour. Note what they touch, avoid and ask about.\n- Pick one \"best practice\" you follow and ask if it fits your users or just looks good.\n\n## Related\n- [[Blink]]: what people say they want vs. how they behave\n- [[Blue Ocean Strategy]]: designing for overlooked customers\n- [[Shoe Dog]]: building a brand around a real customer\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Am I designing for my customers, or for people who look like experts?\"*"
