   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Why Drive-Up ATMs Have Braille\n\n**One-line hook:** Blind people don't drive, so why do drive-through cash machines have Braille on the keypad?\n\n## The Story\nFrank's book is built on questions his students asked about everyday puzzles, and this is one of the most famous. Drive-up ATMs are used by people sitting in cars. Blind people can't drive. So why go to the trouble of putting **Braille** on the keys?\n\nThe answer is simple **cost-benefit** thinking. ATM makers need Braille keypads for walk-up machines anyway, which blind people do use. Making **one standard keypad** for all machines is cheaper than making and stocking two different versions. Adding Braille to a keypad costs almost nothing once you're already producing it. Using the same part everywhere also avoids mix-ups during installation.\n\nWhat looked like a silly waste is actually the **cheaper option**.\n\n## The Lesson\nWhen something seems pointless, look at the **costs and benefits from the producer's side**. Standardizing is often cheaper than customizing, even if some features go unused.\n\n## When to Use This\n- A process or product has a feature that seems pointless\n- You're deciding between one standard design and several custom ones\n- You're tempted to remove something \"nobody uses\"\n\n## Try This\n- Before cutting a \"useless\" feature, ask what it costs to keep and what it would cost to make a version without it.\n- Look for places where one standard version could replace several customized ones.\n\n## Related\n- [[The Toyota Way]]: standardization as a basis for improvement\n- [[The 80/20 Principle]]: knowing which variations actually add value\n- [[Think Like a Rocket Scientist]]: reasoning from basic costs and constraints\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Is this 'waste' actually the cheapest way to do things once I count all the costs?\"*"
