   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Five Minutes With a Scholarship Student\n\n**One-line hook:** Fundraising callers who briefly met one student their work helped went on to raise far more money.\n\n## The Story\nAdam Grant studied a university call center where students phoned alumni to ask for donations. The job was dull and discouraging: most calls ended in rejection, and turnover was high. Pep talks and incentives weren't fixing it.\n\nGrant ran an experiment. One group of callers spent **a few minutes meeting a student whose scholarship had been funded** by the money they raised. The student explained how the scholarship had changed his life. A comparison group didn't have this meeting.\n\nIn the following weeks, the callers who met the scholarship student spent **much more time on the phone and brought in dramatically more money**, a multiple of what they had raised before. Nothing else about the job had changed. What changed was that they could now **see the person their work helped**. Grant uses this to show that givers stay motivated, and avoid burnout, when they see the impact of their giving.\n\n## The Lesson\n**Seeing the impact of your work on a real person is one of the strongest motivators there is.** Meaning beats pep talks.\n\n## When to Use This\n- Your team does repetitive work and feels disconnected from results\n- People seem burned out even though the workload is normal\n- You're designing onboarding or team rituals\n- You yourself have lost track of why your work matters\n\n## Try This\n- Invite one real customer or user to tell your team, in their own words, how your work affected them.\n- Share one short \"impact story\" at the start of a weekly meeting.\n\n## Related\n- [[Start with Why]]: purpose as the driver of commitment\n- [[Extreme Ownership]]: leaders must explain the why behind the mission\n- [[No Rules Rules]]: giving people context so they understand the impact of their work\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Has my team ever met the person their work helps?\"*"
