   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Are Child Car Seats Better Than Seat Belts?\n\n**One-line hook:** Parents spend money and effort on car seats, but crash data suggested that, for kids past toddler age, plain seat belts did about as well at preventing deaths.\n\n## The Story\nLevitt and Dubner start by praising the **seat belt**: a cheap, simple invention that has saved a huge number of lives. Then they ask an awkward question. For children **old enough to use an adult belt** (roughly age two and up), do expensive child car seats save more lives than seat belts?\n\nUsing national U.S. data on fatal crashes, they found that, for preventing **deaths**, car seats showed little or no advantage over seat belts for these older children. Part of the issue is that car seats are hard to install correctly, and many are put in wrong. The authors even paid for **crash tests** with dummies, and the belts did surprisingly well. The test lab staff were reportedly uneasy about the results.\n\nThe point is not \"throw away your car seat.\" It's that a costly, complicated solution was widely assumed to be better, and few people had checked the data against the simple option.\n\n## The Lesson\nDon't assume the **expensive, complex solution** beats the cheap, simple one. Test them against each other using real outcomes.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You're about to buy an expensive tool when a basic one may do\n- Everyone treats a best practice as obviously better\n- A product's value depends on people setting it up correctly\n\n## Try This\n- Compare your fancy option head-to-head with the simplest option on the outcome that matters.\n- Count how often the complex solution is used *correctly* in real life, not in the brochure.\n\n## Related\n- [[Think Like a Rocket Scientist]]: testing assumptions from first principles\n- [[How to Lie with Statistics]]: careful about what the numbers really compare\n- [[Think Again]]: questioning \"everybody knows\" advice\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Is the costly option actually better here, or does it just feel safer?\"*"
