   "verified": false,
   "body": "# The CIA Analyst Who Spoke Up Too Early, Then Won\n\n**One-line hook:** Carmen Medina pushed the CIA to share intelligence online, got sidelined for it, and later succeeded by changing how she spoke up.\n\n## The Story\nGrant tells the story of **Carmen Medina**, a CIA analyst who believed the agency's way of sharing intelligence was badly outdated. Information moved through slow, printed reports. She argued that analysts should share intelligence **online, in real time**, more like the internet.\n\nHer early efforts backfired. She pushed her idea hard before she had the **status and credibility** to make the case, and the reaction from leaders was negative. Grant describes how her career suffered and she was moved aside for a time. Later, after earning more standing in the organization, she raised the ideas again, this time **with more support and in a way that fit the organization's goals**. This time it worked, and the CIA moved toward online sharing tools, including a wiki-style platform for intelligence analysts.\n\nGrant uses Medina's story to show that **speaking up is a skill**. Status matters: people with established credibility have more freedom to challenge norms. He also stresses framing new ideas in terms of **values others already hold**, and finding allies, rather than just declaring the current system broken.\n\n## The Lesson\nBeing right isn't enough. **Earn credibility, find allies, and frame your idea in terms others care about** before you push hard against the status quo.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You see a big problem at work but you're junior\n- Your past attempts to push change were shut down\n- You want your team members to challenge ideas more effectively\n- You're trying to change a slow, traditional organization\n\n## Try This\n- Before your next big proposal, list two respected people who might support it, and talk with them first.\n- Rewrite your pitch to connect the change to a goal your boss already cares about.\n\n## Related\n- [[Outliers]]: how hierarchy affects who feels able to speak\n- [[How to Win Friends and Influence People]]: persuading without making people defensive\n- [[Talking to Strangers]]: why institutions miss warning signs\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Have I earned the credibility to push this yet, and who could carry it with me?\"*"
