   "verified": false,
   "body": "# The Botanist Who Thought Carnegie Was a Great Talker\n\n**One-line hook:** Carnegie barely spoke all evening, and was praised as \"a most interesting conversationalist.\"\n\n## The Story\nAt a dinner party in New York, Dale Carnegie met a well-known botanist. Carnegie knew almost nothing about botany, but he was fascinated, so he **asked questions and listened** for hours, about exotic plants, experiments, and even advice for his own small garden. He ignored the other guests.\n\nAt the end of the evening, the botanist told their host that Carnegie was very stimulating and **\"a most interesting conversationalist.\"** Carnegie had said very little. What he had done was listen with real interest. That made the botanist feel important and enjoy himself, and he gave the credit to Carnegie.\n\nCarnegie's rule from this: **be a good listener and encourage others to talk about themselves.** Most people are much more interested in their own lives and problems than in yours.\n\n## The Lesson\n**People remember how you made them feel, not how much you said.** Curious questions make you more memorable than clever stories.\n\n## When to Use This\n- Networking events where you don't know anyone\n- First meetings with a client, investor or new manager\n- Interviews where you want to build rapport\n- You feel you have \"nothing interesting to say\"\n\n## Try This\n- In your next conversation, ask three follow-up questions before sharing anything about yourself.\n- End meetings by recalling one specific thing the other person said.\n\n## Related\n- [[Give and Take]]: givers build networks by focusing on others\n- [[Talking to Strangers]]: how easily we misread people we don't really listen to\n- [[Think Again]]: asking questions instead of preaching\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"In my last important conversation, who did most of the talking?\"*"
