   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Cirque du Soleil: A Circus Without Animals or Stars\n\n**One-line hook:** In a dying industry, a group of street performers built a hit by removing the very things circuses were known for.\n\n## The Story\nBy the 1980s the traditional circus was a **declining industry**. Kids had video games and other entertainment, animal-rights groups were turning the public against animal acts, and star performers were expensive. Circuses like Ringling Bros. fought each other for a shrinking audience by copying each other's acts.\n\n**Cirque du Soleil**, started in Canada by former street performers, did not try to beat Ringling at its own game. It **eliminated** the costly parts of the circus: animal acts, star performers, and the three-ring format. It kept a few classic elements, like the tent, clowns, and acrobatics, but made them more refined. Then it **added** things from the theater: a storyline or theme for each show, artistic music and dance, and a more elegant setting.\n\nThe result appealed to a **new audience**: adults and corporate clients who would never have bought circus tickets, and who were willing to pay theater-level prices. The authors say Cirque reached revenue in a short time that took the leading traditional circus far longer to reach. It had created a **\"blue ocean\"**: uncontested market space where competition was irrelevant.\n\n## The Lesson\nInstead of fighting rivals in a crowded \"red ocean,\" **change what the industry offers**. Ask what to eliminate, reduce, raise, and create, so you can cut costs and add new value at the same time.\n\n## When to Use This\n- Your industry is shrinking and everyone is copying each other\n- You're competing against a much bigger player with the same offer\n- You're designing a product or event and assume it must include \"standard\" features\n- You want to attract people who currently don't buy at all\n\n## Try This\n- List the features your industry takes for granted. Pick two you could eliminate entirely.\n- Borrow one element from a completely different industry and ask how it would change your offer.\n\n## Related\n- [[Zero to One]]: escape competition instead of winning it\n- [[The Idea Hunter]]: borrow ideas from outside your field\n- [[David and Goliath]]: underdogs win by refusing to play by the giant's rules\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What would happen if I removed the thing everyone in my field thinks is essential?\"*"
