   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Prem Ganapathy: From Washing Dishes to Dosa Plaza\n\n**One-line hook:** He arrived in Mumbai with almost nothing and was cheated on day one; years later he ran a dosa chain.\n\n## The Story\n**Prem Ganapathy** came from a village in Tamil Nadu to Mumbai as a teenager, promised a job by someone who then **abandoned him** with no money in a strange city. He did not speak the local language. He took whatever work he could find, including **washing dishes** at a bakery and small restaurants, and saved bit by bit.\n\nHe then started a small **dosa cart** on the street. What set him apart was not just hard work but **experimenting**: he kept trying new dosa varieties, often using ideas from customers and other cuisines, and built a much larger menu than a typical dosa stall. His focus on cleanliness and variety drew a steady crowd, including college students.\n\nFrom the cart he moved to a small outlet, and then built **Dosa Plaza** into a chain with many outlets. Rashmi Bansal shows that he had no degree or business training, only persistence, learning by doing, and a willingness to keep improving his product.\n\n## The Lesson\nYou don't need a perfect start. **Begin small, keep experimenting, and let customers shape the product.** Steady small gains compound.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You feel behind because others had better starting points\n- You're waiting for the \"right\" moment to start\n- A small business or project feels stuck\n- You've had a big setback early on\n\n## Try This\n- Start the smallest version of your idea this week, even if it's a \"cart\" version.\n- Ask your users what they'd want next, and test one new idea every week.\n\n## Related\n- [[The 1% Rule]]: small daily improvements add up\n- [[Hidden Potential]]: growth matters more than starting point\n- [[The Greatest Salesman in the World]]: persistence through hard beginnings\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What's my 'dosa cart', the tiny version I can start tomorrow?\"*"
