   "body": "# Hafid's Unsold Robe: The Failure That Proved He Was Ready\n\n**One-line hook:** A young camel boy failed his first sales test, gave away the robe instead, and that is exactly why his master trusted him.\n\n## The Story\nHafid is a poor camel boy who works for Pathros, a great merchant. Hafid wants to become a salesman so he can become rich and marry the woman he loves. Pathros tests him: he gives Hafid a **single fine robe** and sends him to the village of Bethlehem to sell it.\n\nHafid tries for days and fails. Then he finds a newborn baby in a cave near an inn, sleeping in the cold beside a stable. Moved by pity, Hafid **wraps the infant in the robe** he was supposed to sell. He returns to the caravan ashamed, believing he has failed. But Pathros had seen a bright star following the boy. He recognizes it as the **sign he had been waiting for**: the one who should receive his secret. The robe wasn't sold, but the gift showed Hafid's heart.\n\nPathros then gives Hafid a chest holding **ten scrolls** containing the principles of success, along with money to start his own business. Hafid uses them and becomes the greatest salesman in the world.\n\n## The Lesson\n**Character matters more than one sale.** What looks like failure in the short term can be the clearest proof of the qualities that lead to long-term success.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You missed a target and feel like a failure\n- You're tempted to cut corners to close a deal\n- You're judging a junior person on a single result\n- You wonder whether kindness has a place in business\n\n## Try This\n- After a \"failed\" attempt, list what it revealed about your values and skills, not just the result.\n- When judging someone new, look at how they behaved under pressure, not only at the outcome.\n\n## Related\n- [[Give and Take]]: givers can end up at the top\n- [[The Obstacle Is the Way]]: turning a setback into something useful\n- [[Shoe Dog]]: early struggles that shaped a founder\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What did my last 'failure' reveal about me that a success wouldn't have?\"*"
