   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Hansell vs. LeMay: The Idealist Who Was Fired and the Pragmatist Who Replaced Him\n\n**One-line hook:** One general stuck to his principles and got poor results; the other got results and paid a terrible moral price.\n\n## The Story\n**Haywood Hansell** was a true believer in the Bomber Mafia's dream: precision bombing of key targets from high altitude, sparing civilians. In late 1944 he commanded the B-29 bombers flying from the Mariana Islands against Japan. But conditions were brutal. Clouds covered targets, and over Japan the bombers ran into the **jet stream**, extremely fast high-altitude winds that threw their aim off. Raid after raid failed to destroy the targets.\n\nWashington lost patience. In early 1945 Hansell was replaced by **Curtis LeMay**, a tough, practical commander focused on results. LeMay abandoned high-altitude precision bombing and switched to **low-level night raids with firebombs** on Japanese cities. Militarily, the change \"worked.\" Gladwell treats this as a real dilemma: Hansell was loyal to an ideal and failed at the job; LeMay succeeded at the job by doing something horrific. Gladwell openly wrestles with which man was right.\n\n## The Lesson\nPrinciples and results can pull in opposite directions. **Be clear about which you're optimising for**, and honest about the cost of each choice.\n\n## When to Use This\n- Your values-based approach isn't producing results and pressure is mounting\n- You're replacing a leader and choosing between an idealist and a pragmatist\n- A shortcut would hit the target but feels wrong\n- You're judging a past decision that \"worked\" at a high cost\n\n## Try This\n- Write down your non-negotiables before the pressure hits, so you're not deciding them in a crisis.\n- When results fall short, ask whether the principle is wrong or only the method. Change the method first.\n\n## Related\n- [[Start with Why]]: holding on to purpose when tactics fail\n- [[Extreme Ownership]]: owning both the mission and the methods\n- [[Think Again]]: knowing when to rethink a belief and when to keep it\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Am I Hansell or LeMay right now, and what would the other one tell me?\"*"
