   "verified": false,
   "body": "# How a Debate Champion Beat a Computer by Agreeing First\n\n**One-line hook:** Facing an AI armed with mountains of evidence, a world-class debater won over the audience by starting with what he agreed with.\n\n## The Story\nIn 2019, debate champion **Harish Natarajan** faced **IBM's Project Debater**, an AI system that could draw on a huge amount of research. The topic was whether preschools should be subsidized. The computer argued for subsidies; Harish argued against.\n\nThe AI came armed with a flood of studies and statistics. Harish didn't try to match it fact for fact. Instead, he **started by finding common ground**, acknowledging points where he and his opponent agreed, such as the importance of helping disadvantaged children. Then he focused on **a few strong arguments** rather than many. Audience votes suggested he moved more people toward his side.\n\nGrant uses this to show how expert negotiators and debaters differ from average ones. Skilled persuaders **look for agreement, ask questions, and use fewer arguments**, because piling on reasons dilutes your best point and makes people defensive. Persuasion works less like a war and more like a **dance**.\n\n## The Lesson\nTo change someone's mind, start with what you agree on, then make a few strong points instead of many weak ones.\n\n## When to Use This\n- Negotiating with someone who sees you as an opponent\n- Pitching an idea to a skeptical boss or client\n- Disagreeing with a teammate without damaging the relationship\n- You're tempted to win by burying the other side in data\n\n## Try This\n- Open your next tough conversation by stating one thing you genuinely agree with them on.\n- Cut your argument down to your two strongest reasons and drop the rest.\n\n## Related\n- [[How to Win Friends and Influence People]]: get the other person saying \"yes\" early\n- [[Give and Take]]: persuading through humility and genuine interest\n- [[Talking to Strangers]]: why understanding the other person matters more than winning\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What do I genuinely agree with them about, and which two points actually matter most?\"*"
