   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Intel Israel's Rebellion: Why Faster Chips Were the Wrong Goal\n\n**One-line hook:** An Israeli team argued with Intel headquarters until it changed the company's whole strategy.\n\n## The Story\nFor years, Intel competed on **clock speed**: each new chip was faster than the last. But faster chips used more power and ran hotter, which was a problem for laptops. Engineers at **Intel's Israel team** argued that the future was mobile computing, and that Intel should focus on **chips that used less power** while still performing well.\n\nSenor and Singer describe how the Israelis pushed this view hard and repeatedly with Intel's U.S. leadership, who were committed to the speed race. The Israeli culture of **arguing openly with bosses**, which the book connects to Israeli **chutzpah** and to an army culture where junior people challenge senior ones, meant they didn't back down after the first \"no.\"\n\nIntel eventually adopted their approach. The result was the **Centrino** platform for laptops, which became a big success and helped change how Intel thought about chip design.\n\n## The Lesson\n**Sometimes the most valuable person is the one who keeps challenging the official plan.** Cultures that allow pushback catch strategic mistakes earlier.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You believe your organization is chasing the wrong metric\n- You're a manager and wonder if your team would challenge you\n- You're working with teams from more direct or more polite cultures\n- You need to pitch an unpopular idea to leadership\n\n## Try This\n- Build your case with data and a clear customer need, then keep presenting it rather than dropping it after one \"no.\"\n- Ask your team: \"What are we optimizing that customers no longer care about?\"\n\n## Related\n- [[Outliers]]: power distance and why some teams don't speak up\n- [[Think Again]]: rethinking long-held assumptions\n- [[Chip War]]: the history and strategy of the chip industry\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What is my organization racing toward that no longer matters, and who's willing to say so?\"*"
