   "verified": false,
   "body": "# The \"Crazy Idea\": Phil Knight Walks into a Japanese Shoe Company\n\n**One-line hook:** A young graduate with no company and no product invented a business name on the spot to win a meeting in Japan.\n\n## The Story\nAt Stanford business school, Phil Knight wrote a paper arguing that **Japanese running shoes** could do to the American market what Japanese cameras had done: offer quality at a lower price and take on the German leaders. He called it his **\"Crazy Idea.\"**\n\nIn 1962, at 24, he set off on a trip around the world. In Japan, he went to Kobe and talked his way into a meeting with **Onitsuka**, the maker of **Tiger** running shoes. When the executives asked what company he represented, Knight had none. He made one up on the spot: **Blue Ribbon Sports**. He told them he wanted to distribute Tigers in the United States. The executives agreed to send samples.\n\nWhen the shoes arrived, Knight sent a pair to his old track coach at the University of Oregon, **Bill Bowerman**, hoping for an order. Instead Bowerman offered to become his **partner**. They each put in a small amount of money, and Knight started selling shoes out of the **trunk of his car** at track meets. That business became Nike.\n\n## The Lesson\n**Sometimes you have to act before you feel ready.** Showing up with confidence, and a clear idea of what you want, can open doors that credentials never would.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You have an idea but no title, team, or resources yet\n- You're about to talk yourself out of a bold meeting or ask\n- You're starting a side project and waiting for \"the right time\"\n- You need a first partner or mentor\n\n## Try This\n- Write your own \"Crazy Idea\" in one paragraph: the gap you see and why now.\n- Send one bold ask this week to someone who could help, and show up as if the business already exists.\n\n## Related\n- [[The Everything Store]]: Bezos leaves a safe job to start Amazon\n- [[Originals]]: how original thinkers get started\n- [[Screw It, Let's Do It]]: Branson's bias toward action\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What would I do this week if I acted like my idea already existed?\"*"
