   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Korean Air & the Plane Crashes Caused by Politeness\n\n**One-line hook:** Planes crashed because junior pilots were too polite to tell the captain he was wrong.\n\n## The Story\nIn the 1990s Korean Air had one of the worst crash rates in the industry. Gladwell argues that the cause was usually not bad flying. It was **communication shaped by culture**. In cockpit recordings, first officers who saw danger coming hinted at it (\"mitigated speech\") rather than saying it outright. In a high **power-distance** culture, telling the captain plainly that he was making a mistake felt unthinkable.\n\nThe turnaround came when the airline brought in outside help from Delta and made **English the cockpit language**. Speaking English gave crews a way to step out of the deference built into Korean. Korean Air later became a safe airline.\n\n## The Lesson\nFailures are often **communication failures that look like skill failures**. The hierarchy in a room can quietly overrule the facts in that room.\n\n## When to Use This\n- A team keeps missing problems that someone \"must have seen\"\n- You're the senior person and nobody pushes back on you\n- You're working across cultures or with very hierarchical organizations\n- You're designing a process for escalation or safety\n\n## Try This\n- Make pushback easy: \"Tell me the one thing I'm getting wrong.\"\n- Set a shared, neutral protocol (like checklists or a \"stop the line\" rule) so speaking up doesn't depend on courage.\n\n## Related\n- [[Toyota Way]]: the andon cord. Any worker can stop the line.\n- [[Extreme Ownership]]: \"Check the ego\" and leading up the chain\n- [[Think Again]]: building a challenge network\n- [[No Rules Rules]]: Netflix's culture of candor\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Where in my team is someone seeing the problem but only hinting at it?\"*"
