   "body": "# Chris Langan vs. Robert Oppenheimer: Why Raw Genius Isn't Enough\n\n**One-line hook:** The man with one of the highest IQs ever measured lost his college scholarship over a form, while a physicist who tried to poison his tutor talked his way out of trouble.\n\n## The Story\nChris Langan has an IQ far above Einstein's, yet he grew up poor in a chaotic home in Montana. At Reed College he lost his scholarship because his mother **never filled out a financial-aid form**. Later, at Montana State, he asked to move his classes to later in the day because of a transport problem. The administrator said no, and Langan didn't know how to argue his case. He dropped out and ended up working as a bouncer on a horse ranch, building theories on his own.\n\nRobert Oppenheimer, as a graduate student at Cambridge, tried to poison his tutor. Instead of being expelled or prosecuted, he **negotiated his way into probation**. Years later, with far less obvious qualifications than other candidates, he persuaded General Leslie Groves to put him in charge of the Manhattan Project.\n\nThe difference, Gladwell argues, was **practical intelligence**. This is knowing what to say, to whom, and when, to get what you want. It isn't innate. Citing sociologist Annette Lareau, he shows that middle-class parents practice **\"concerted cultivation\"**, teaching kids to question adults and push for themselves. Kids from poorer homes are often taught to defer to authority.\n\n## The Lesson\nAnalytical brilliance gets you to the door. **Knowing how to talk to institutions** gets you through it, and that skill is learned, mostly at home.\n\n## When to Use This\n- A brilliant teammate keeps losing arguments they should win\n- You're judging candidates and over-weighting raw intelligence\n- You hit a bureaucratic \"no\" and are tempted to accept it\n- You're deciding what to teach your kids beyond grades\n\n## Try This\n- The next time an institution says no, ask \"What would need to be true for this to be a yes?\"\n- Mentor someone from a less privileged background on how to self-advocate. It's a teachable skill.\n\n## Related\n- [[How to Win Friends and Influence People]]: the practical playbook for talking to people in power\n- [[Hidden Potential]]: character skills matter more than raw talent\n- [[Talking to Strangers]]: how first impressions and context shape the way others judge us\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Am I losing this because I'm wrong, or because I haven't learned how to make the ask?\"*"
