   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Why Maruti Chose the Smallest Suitor: Suzuki\n\n**One-line hook:** India's biggest car company was built with a partner that was not the biggest, but was the hungriest.\n\n## The Story\nIn the early 1980s the government-owned Maruti Udyog needed a foreign partner to build a modern small car. The team talked to several established car makers. Many treated India as a side project. They were cautious, offered limited technology, and did not want to put much at stake.\n\nSuzuki was a smaller player in the car world, but its chief, **Osamu Suzuki**, took India seriously. He moved fast, engaged personally, and was willing to take an **equity stake** in Maruti, so Suzuki would win only if Maruti won. Bhargava describes how that **commitment mattered more than size or brand**. Suzuki's stake grew over the years, and the partnership lasted for decades.\n\nThe choice worked because Suzuki's interests were tied to Maruti's success. For Suzuki, India became a core market, not a side project. That made it willing to transfer know-how, train Indian engineers in Japan, and stay through hard times.\n\n## The Lesson\nWhen choosing a partner, **pick the one with the most skin in the game**, not the most famous name. A partner for whom you are central will work harder than a giant for whom you are a footnote.\n\n## When to Use This\n- Choosing between a big-name vendor and a smaller, more committed one\n- Picking a co-founder, investor or agency\n- Structuring a deal so both sides win or lose together\n- Deciding which client or employer will actually invest in you\n\n## Try This\n- Ask each candidate partner: \"How important is this deal to you, compared with your other work?\" Then check whether their actions match the answer.\n- Build shared ownership or shared rewards into agreements so incentives line up.\n\n## Related\n- [[Give and Take]]: building partnerships where both sides add value\n- [[The Logic of Life]]: incentives explain behavior better than promises\n- [[David and Goliath]]: the smaller player can be the stronger choice\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Which of my options cares about this as much as I do?\"*"
