   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Why Milk Comes in Boxes and Soda in Cans\n\n**One-line hook:** The shape of a container tells you a surprising amount about costs and trade-offs.\n\n## The Story\nFrank asks why **soft drinks** come in round cans and bottles while **milk** usually comes in rectangular cartons. Rectangles use shelf space more efficiently; round containers waste the gaps between them. So why aren't soda cans square too?\n\nThe answer is about **where the costs fall**. Milk must be kept in **refrigerated cases**, and refrigerated space is expensive to run. Packing milk tightly in rectangles saves a lot of costly cooled space. Soft drinks are mostly stored on ordinary, unrefrigerated shelves, where space is cheaper. So for soda, other benefits of a round can matter more: it's **comfortable to hold and drink from**, and cylinders handle the pressure of carbonation well. People also often drink soda straight from the can, while milk is usually poured into a glass.\n\nThe difference isn't random. Each shape is the best answer to a **different set of costs and benefits**.\n\n## The Lesson\nDesign choices that look arbitrary often reflect **hidden trade-offs**. Find the most expensive constraint, and the design usually makes sense.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You're copying a design or process from another context\n- You're deciding how to package, store or ship something\n- You see a competitor do something \"strange\" and want to understand why\n\n## Try This\n- List the costliest constraint in your situation (space, time, people) and design around it first.\n- Before copying a \"best practice,\" check whether your costs and benefits are the same as theirs.\n\n## Related\n- [[The Goal]]: designing around the most important constraint\n- [[The Toyota Way]]: careful thinking about space, flow and waste\n- [[Think Like a Rocket Scientist]]: first-principles reasoning about design\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What's the most expensive thing in this situation, and is my design built around it?\"*"
