   "body": "# \"Dimension Six\" vs. \"Nike\": A Brand Born Under Deadline\n\n**One-line hook:** The world's most famous sports brand got its name in a rush, and its founder wasn't sure he liked it.\n\n## The Story\nWhen Phil Knight's company needed its own brand, separate from the Japanese Tiger shoes it had been selling, the team had to pick a name **fast**, because the factory needed it for the shoes and boxes. Knight's own favorite was **\"Dimension Six.\"** His colleagues didn't like it.\n\nKnight's first employee, **Jeff Johnson**, came up with **\"Nike,\"** the Greek goddess of victory, saying the idea came to him in a dream. Knight wasn't convinced, but with the deadline here, he went with it, saying something like \"maybe it'll grow on us.\"\n\nThe logo had a similar story. Knight hired a design student, **Carolyn Davidson**, for a small fee to design a mark. She produced what became the **Swoosh**. Knight's reaction was lukewarm: he didn't love it, but hoped it would grow on him. Both the name and the logo became among the most valuable brand assets in the world.\n\n## The Lesson\n**Don't let perfection stall a launch.** A good-enough choice made on time can become great through what you build behind it, and the founder's taste isn't always right.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You're stuck debating a name, logo, or detail\n- You're the boss and your favorite option keeps losing\n- A deadline forces a decision before you feel sure\n- You're overvaluing first impressions of a new idea\n\n## Try This\n- Set a decision deadline for small but sticky choices, and stick to it.\n- When your team prefers an option you don't love, ask: \"Is my objection about the work or my ego?\"\n\n## Related\n- [[Ego Is the Enemy]]: letting go of being the smartest in the room\n- [[Blink]]: when first reactions mislead us\n- [[The Airbnb Story]]: another brand built from scrappy beginnings\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Am I delaying a decision because it's wrong, or because it's not perfect?\"*"
