   "verified": false,
   "body": "# How an American Supermarket Inspired Toyota's Pull System\n\n**One-line hook:** Toyota's famous just-in-time system borrowed its key idea from grocery store shelves.\n\n## The Story\nIn the years after World War II, Toyota was small, short on cash, and couldn't afford to fill warehouses with parts. American car companies used **mass production**: make huge batches and push them into inventory. **Taiichi Ohno** wanted a way to make only what was needed, when it was needed.\n\nHe found the idea in the **American supermarket**. A supermarket doesn't guess how much milk each customer will buy and push it into their homes. It keeps a small amount on the shelf, and when customers take items, the store **restocks just what was taken**. Ohno applied this inside the factory: each process \"shops\" for the parts it needs from the step before, and that step only makes enough to **replace what was pulled**. Simple cards called **kanban** signal what to make next.\n\nThis **pull system** cut inventory, exposed problems that piles of stock used to hide, and became a core part of the Toyota Production System.\n\n## The Lesson\n**Make things in response to real demand, not forecasts.** Extra inventory hides problems; less of it forces you to fix them.\n\n## When to Use This\n- Work piles up between teams and nobody knows what's really needed\n- Your team produces reports, features, or content nobody uses yet\n- You want a new idea and keep looking only inside your own industry\n- Planning cycles are long and keep missing reality\n\n## Try This\n- Limit work-in-progress: only start a new task when the next person is ready to \"pull\" it.\n- Look at a business in a totally different field and ask how it solves your exact problem.\n\n## Related\n- [[It's Not Luck]]: central warehouses refilled by actual sales\n- [[The Idea Hunter]]: finding ideas by looking outside your field\n- [[The Goal]]: why excess inventory is a warning sign\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Am I pushing work out based on guesses, or letting real demand pull it?\"*"
