   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Operation Flood: Using Foreign Surplus to Build Indian Dairies\n\n**One-line hook:** Free food aid could have crushed India's dairy farmers, so Kurien sold it to fund their growth.\n\n## The Story\nEuropean countries had large **surpluses** of dairy products such as milk powder and butter oil. Giving it to India as aid sounded generous. But Kurien saw the danger: cheap foreign milk flooding Indian cities would **undercut local farmers** and kill the dairy industry before it grew.\n\nHis answer was **Operation Flood**. The donated products were not given away. They were recombined into milk and **sold** in Indian cities at fair prices, so local farmers were not undercut. The money raised was used to build **dairy cooperatives, processing plants and supply links** that connected village milk producers to big city markets.\n\nOver time, the project helped make India one of the world's largest milk producers. Kurien turned a gift that could have caused **dependence** into **investment** in farmers' own capacity.\n\n## The Lesson\nHelp that ignores incentives can hurt the people it means to help. **Redesign the flow of resources** so it builds local strength instead of replacing it.\n\n## When to Use This\n- Receiving a free resource that might make your team dependent\n- Designing aid, subsidies or discounts\n- A big player offers help that could kill smaller players\n- Thinking through the second-order effects of a \"generous\" policy\n\n## Try This\n- Before accepting help, ask: \"What does this do to local incentives in two years?\"\n- Look for ways to turn one-time help into lasting capacity (training, tools, infrastructure).\n\n## Related\n- [[SuperFreakonomics]]: good intentions with bad side effects\n- [[Naked Economics]]: how prices and incentives shape markets\n- [[Rebooting India]]: designing public systems that reach people directly\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Is this help building our strength or replacing it?\"*"
