   "verified": false,
   "body": "# The Screen That Let Orchestras Finally Hear Women\n\n**One-line hook:** Top orchestras were sure they hired purely on talent, until a simple curtain changed who got hired.\n\n## The Story\nFor a long time, classical orchestras were overwhelmingly male. Conductors and judges believed they could simply **hear** who was best, and many sincerely thought men played better.\n\nGladwell tells the story of trombonist **Abbie Conant**, who auditioned for the Munich Philharmonic behind a screen. The judges were so impressed they stopped the auditions early. When she stepped out, they were shocked she was a woman. Afterward she faced resistance and unfair treatment, despite having blown the judges away.\n\nAs orchestras adopted **screens during auditions**, which hide who is playing, they began hiring many more women. The judges hadn't been lying about listening carefully. Their **unconscious first impressions**, from seeing the player, appearance, and even how they held the instrument, were quietly shaping what they \"heard.\" Gladwell's point: you can't always fix snap judgments by trying harder. Sometimes you have to **redesign the situation** so bias never enters.\n\n## The Lesson\nBiased snap judgments often feel like objective expertise. The fix is often structural: remove the information that triggers the bias.\n\n## When to Use This\n- Designing a hiring or promotion process\n- Evaluating proposals, pitches, or work samples\n- Your team looks very similar despite \"hiring the best\"\n- Judges or reviewers insist they're unbiased\n\n## Try This\n- Review work samples or test results before seeing names, photos, or schools.\n- Agree on scoring criteria before evaluating anyone, and score independently.\n\n## Related\n- [[Outliers]]: how hidden advantages shape who looks \"talented\"\n- [[Hidden Potential]]: spotting ability that first impressions miss\n- [[Originals]]: how organizations miss good ideas from unexpected people\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What could I hide in this evaluation so I judge only what actually matters?\"*"
