   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Roger vs. Tiger: The Late Specializer Who Won Anyway\n\n**One-line hook:** Tiger Woods was swinging a club as a toddler. Roger Federer tried a dozen sports first, and still became one of the greatest ever.\n\n## The Story\nTiger Woods is the famous head-start story. His father put a golf club in his hands before he could walk properly, and he was showing off his swing on TV as a small child. Tiger became the poster child for the idea that **early, narrow, intense practice** is the path to greatness.\n\nRoger Federer's path looked completely different. As a kid he played many sports, including skiing, wrestling, swimming, skateboarding, basketball, handball, badminton and soccer, alongside tennis. His mother was a tennis coach but **chose not to coach him**. Federer focused on tennis only later than his peers, and even turned down a promotion to a higher-level group because he wanted to stay with his friends. He went on to dominate the sport.\n\nEpstein argues that **Federer's path is the more common one** among elite performers. Most top athletes have a **\"sampling period\"**. They try a range of activities, build broad physical skills, learn what they enjoy and are good at, and *then* specialize. Tiger's story is memorable, but it isn't typical.\n\n## The Lesson\nA late start is not a fatal flaw. **Breadth first, then depth** often builds a better fit and a stronger foundation than an early, narrow head start.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You feel \"behind\" because peers specialized earlier\n- You're deciding whether to switch fields or roles\n- You're planning a child's activities and feel pressure to specialize early\n- You're hiring and dismissing candidates with \"winding\" résumés\n\n## Try This\n- Run small experiments, such as a side project, a course or a short rotation, before committing to a specialty.\n- When judging a career path, ask \"what did they learn from each turn?\" rather than \"why so many turns?\"\n\n## Related\n- [[Outliers]]: the 10,000-hour rule, the head-start argument that Range challenges\n- [[Hidden Potential]]: growth depends more on the path than on an early start\n- [[Think Like a Rocket Scientist]]: drawing on many fields to solve problems\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Am I measuring myself against a Tiger story when I'm actually on a Roger path?\"*"
