   "body": "# Kunwer Sachdev: Finding a Business in Broken Inverters\n\n**One-line hook:** He went from selling pens to running a cable TV business, then built an inverter company because the ones he used kept failing.\n\n## The Story\n**Kunwer Sachdev** did not follow a straight path. He grew up in Delhi in a modest family and started out in sales, including **selling pens**. He then moved into the **cable TV** business, where he learned about electronics and dealing with equipment.\n\nIn Delhi, **power cuts** were common, and many homes used inverters for backup. Sachdev noticed that the inverters available were often **poor quality** and broke down. Instead of just complaining, he saw it as an opportunity. Using what he had learned, he began making his own inverters and built **Su-Kam**, which grew into a major Indian power backup brand.\n\nBansal uses his story to show how entrepreneurs without formal business training often **connect the dots** from earlier, seemingly unrelated jobs. Sales taught him customers; cable TV taught him electronics; daily frustration showed him the problem.\n\n## The Lesson\nYour next opportunity is often **a product you're frustrated with**. Earlier \"random\" jobs give you the skills to fix it.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You feel your career path looks scattered\n- Searching for a business or project idea\n- You keep running into a poor-quality product or service\n- Deciding whether a side skill is \"wasted\"\n\n## Try This\n- List three products or services that annoy you every week. Which could you improve?\n- Write down skills from each past job, and look for a combination few people have.\n\n## Related\n- [[Range]]: varied experience can be an advantage\n- [[The Idea Hunter]]: spotting ideas in everyday life\n- [[Shoe Dog]]: building a company from a product you care about\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What keeps breaking in my world that I know how to fix?\"*"
