   "body": "# Alex Rogo's Trap: The Better He Does, the Faster His Companies Get Sold\n\n**One-line hook:** Alex is told to fix three companies so the board can sell them, and doing his job well might cost his people their jobs.\n\n## The Story\nIn the sequel to The Goal, Alex Rogo has been promoted to run a diversified group of three companies: a **cosmetics** company, a **steam equipment** maker (Pressure Steam), and a **printing** company. The board decides to sell the group to raise cash. Alex is caught in a painful conflict. If he improves the companies, they become more attractive and **sell faster**, and new owners may cut the managers and workers who trusted him. If he doesn't improve them, they'll be sold cheaply anyway, with the same risk.\n\nInstead of picking the least-bad option, Alex uses Jonah's **thinking processes**. He maps the conflict as an **\"evaporating cloud\"**: both sides share a common goal, and each side rests on hidden assumptions. By questioning those assumptions, he looks for a third path. His aim becomes making the companies so strong and profitable that keeping them, or selling them on good terms that protect his people, becomes the better deal.\n\n## The Lesson\n**Most \"either/or\" dilemmas rest on an assumption you haven't questioned.** Write down both sides and what each one needs, and the way out often shows up.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You're stuck choosing between two bad options\n- Your boss's goal seems to conflict with your team's interests\n- A negotiation feels like pure win-lose\n- You're doing well but worry it'll backfire on you\n\n## Try This\n- Draw the conflict: the shared goal, the two needs, the two opposing actions. Then list the assumptions behind each arrow.\n- Pick the weakest assumption and ask: \"What would have to be true for this to be false?\"\n\n## Related\n- [[The Goal]]: the first book, where Alex learns from Jonah\n- [[Give and Take]]: finding outcomes that serve everyone, not just yourself\n- [[Think Again]]: questioning assumptions you've treated as facts\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What hidden assumption makes this feel like an either/or?\"*"
