   "body": "# Adam Grant Passed on Warby Parker, and Got the Lesson Backwards\n\n**One-line hook:** Grant turned down investing in Warby Parker because the founders were playing it safe, and that caution was exactly why they succeeded.\n\n## The Story\nGrant opens Originals with a confession. A group of Wharton students pitched him their idea: sell glasses **online**, at a much lower price than the big eyewear brands. Grant liked the idea but **declined to invest**, partly because the founders didn't seem fully committed. They were still in school, they had **lined up internships or jobs as backups**, and they spent a long time preparing before launching.\n\nTo Grant, that looked like a lack of commitment. Real entrepreneurs, he thought, should go all in. The company was **Warby Parker**, and it became a huge success. Grant calls passing on it one of his worst financial decisions.\n\nHe later realized he had it wrong. Many successful originals **don't take extreme risks**. They **balance risk**: taking a bold bet in one area while staying cautious in others, such as keeping a day job while the new venture proves itself. Grant cites research suggesting that entrepreneurs who kept their day jobs while starting a business were **less likely to fail** than those who quit. Feeling secure in one part of life gives people the freedom to be original in another.\n\n## The Lesson\nBeing original doesn't require betting everything. **Hedge in one area so you can be bold in another.**\n\n## When to Use This\n- You want to start a side business but can't afford to quit your job\n- You're judging a founder or employee by how much risk they take\n- You think bold ideas require bold, all-in moves\n- You're nervous about a career change\n\n## Try This\n- Make a \"risk portfolio\": list where your life is stable and where you're taking a bet. Keep at least one area safe.\n- Test your idea on nights and weekends before deciding whether to go full time.\n\n## Related\n- [[Zero to One]]: making bold bets on new ideas\n- [[Shoe Dog]]: Phil Knight kept an accounting job while building Nike\n- [[Think Like a Freak]]: rethinking assumptions about risk and quitting\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Where can I stay safe so I can afford to be bold somewhere else?\"*"
