   "verified": false,
   "body": "# Who Really Profits From Your Commuter Coffee\n\n**One-line hook:** You pay a lot for a coffee at a busy station, but most of the extra money doesn't go to the coffee shop.\n\n## The Story\nHarford starts with a cup of coffee bought near a busy London commuter station. The price is high, and the cost of beans, milk and cup is tiny. So who gets the rest?\n\nHis answer is **scarcity power**. The coffee shop sits on the exact path of thousands of rushed commuters, and there is only so much space in that spot. That **location** is what's scarce, not coffee or baristas. Anyone can hire staff or buy an espresso machine, so those can't earn special profits. The spot by the station exit can't be copied, so the **landlord** who owns it can charge a rent that captures much of the extra profit. The coffee shop ends up paying for the privilege of being there.\n\nHarford uses this to explain a general rule. In any business, ask **which input is truly scarce**. Whoever controls that scarce thing has the bargaining power, and over time collects most of the profit.\n\n## The Lesson\nProfits flow to whoever controls **the scarce resource**, which is often not the business that serves the customer.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You're judging whether a business or role has real pricing power\n- You're negotiating with a supplier, platform or landlord\n- You wonder why a \"good\" business still has thin margins\n\n## Try This\n- Map your value chain and circle the input that's hardest to replace. Who owns it?\n- Before you invest in a position, ask whether competitors can easily copy it.\n\n## Related\n- [[Zero to One]]: Thiel on building something others can't copy\n- [[The Four]]: how tech giants control the scarce choke points\n- [[Chip War]]: control of a scarce input as a source of power\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"What is truly scarce here, and who owns it?\"*"
