   "body": "# Why Your Boss Is \"Overpaid\" (On Purpose)\n\n**One-line hook:** The CEO's giant salary may not be a reward for the CEO at all. It's bait for everyone below.\n\n## The Story\nWhy does a chief executive earn so much more than the vice presidents just below, even when the work doesn't seem that different? Harford explains **tournament theory**.\n\nIn many organizations, it's hard to measure exactly how well each person is doing. But it's easy to tell **who is doing better than whom**. So companies run a contest. The top job comes with a huge prize, and that prize is meant to motivate the **many people competing for it**. The CEO's pay is like the prize money in a golf tournament. It's not about how much harder the winner played on the final hole. It's about making every player try hard all season.\n\nHarford also shows the **downside**. Tournaments encourage people to take big gambles (because coming second pays little), and they can reward **sabotaging rivals** instead of helping colleagues. Where teamwork matters most, a big, all-or-nothing prize can do real damage.\n\n## The Lesson\nBig gaps in pay or status often work as **prizes to drive competition** below. That can spur effort, but it can also breed risk-taking and backstabbing.\n\n## When to Use This\n- You're designing a promotion or bonus system\n- Your team competes when you need it to cooperate\n- You're deciding whether to chase a \"winner-take-all\" promotion\n\n## Try This\n- If teamwork matters, reward team results and narrow the gap between first and second place.\n- Before entering a tournament, ask what the \"losers\" get and whether you're okay with that.\n\n## Related\n- [[Freakonomics]]: the drug gang's tournament structure, where foot soldiers chase a few big prizes\n- [[No Rules Rules]]: Netflix's approach to pay and avoiding internal competition\n- [[Give and Take]]: what happens to givers and takers in competitive cultures\n\n## Aha Prompt\n*\"Is this reward helping people work harder, or teaching them to beat each other?\"*"
